"""
Continuous peak-hold approximation of the original event-based `PeakSampler`.

Ported from `ShipSIM.Components.DataProcessing.PeakSampler`.

The Modelica original used a `ZeroCrossing(der(u))` event with `when … then y = u; end when`
to capture the value of `u` at every local extremum. Dyad does not (yet) expose
Modelica-style discrete `when` events, so this port uses a *continuous* peak-hold
filter:

```math
\dot y = \max(\dot u, -\\alpha (y - u))
```

i.e. the output `y` rises at the same rate as `u` whenever `u` is increasing, and decays
slowly toward `u` when `u` is decreasing. With a large `alpha` the filter approximates
the running maximum; with `alpha = 0` it captures only the most-recent rising peak. The
default `alpha = 0` is closest to the upstream behavior (peak-hold), but cannot reset
between successive peaks the way the discrete original could.

Limitations vs upstream:
- Captures only running maxima, not the alternating max/min of the upstream model.
- Cannot reset between peaks without external intervention. To track each peak
  individually, post-process the output of this sampler externally (e.g. a Julia
  rolling-window max).
"""
component PeakSampler
  "Decay rate toward u when u is decreasing. Set to 0 for pure peak-hold."
  parameter alpha::Real = 0
  "Initial output value at t = 0. Pick > min(u) to keep the filter armed."
  parameter y_start::Real = 0

  u = RealInput()
  y = RealOutput()

  variable y_state::Real
relations
  initial y_state = y_start

  # Continuous peak-hold. ifelse picks the rising-rate of u when y is below u, otherwise
  # decays toward u with rate alpha.
  der(y_state) = ifelse(u > y_state, (u - y_state) * 1e3, -alpha * (y_state - u))
  y = y_state
end
